  "subject": "💡 Axios Finish Line:  Take a walk!",
  "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:29:29 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🍂 Plus: Riverside retreat | Monday, October 26, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 26, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Hello, Monday! Smart Brevity™ count: 406 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Think on your feet \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Your next great idea probably won't come from sitting on the couch. You may need to take a walk, preferably without a destination, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. \n\n 🚶 Why it matters: Walking doesn't just benefit your physical and mental health. Research shows it can unlock divergent thinking — the ability to generate new and original ideas that's essential to creativity. 🖼️ The big picture: Masters of their craft, from Albert Einstein to Stephen King and Virginia Woolf, all credited walking with fueling their creativity. Now, science is backing them up, The Washington Post's Richard Sima reports . \n\n A recent analysis of 23 studies found that walking significantly improved people's ability to come up with novel ideas. But there's a catch: Walking was less effective at convergent thinking — the process of evaluating ideas and deciding which are worth pursuing. That may require a different approach, including collaboration with others. 🔎 Zoom in: How you walk also matters. \n\n A 2022 study found that people who were allowed to roam freely generated more novel ideas than those confined to a predetermined walking path. Researchers suspect that movement gives our minds the space to wander, helping us break free from rigid thought patterns. In a separate 2024 study , researchers tracked 157 young adults over five days and found that those who took more steps before a creativity test came up with more original answers. 🎶 The intrigue: The connection goes beyond brainstorming. \n\n People who are more physically active also tend to engage more in creative pursuits — like cooking, music and performing arts — and report more creative accomplishments. 💡 The bottom line: Next time you're in a rut, get up and wander, even if it's in the comfort of your own home. (For me, that's three rooms, but whatever works!) \n\n Give yourself space to daydream, but keep your phone or a notepad handy to record flashes of inspiration. Once you've got your idea , you'll probably need to take a seat to see it through. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🍁 Parting shot: Pull up a chair \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Craig Woodward We also endorse taking a seat. Reader Craig Woodward captured this lucky, un-staged shot along a river in northern Alabama.\n\n 🍂 \"The word 'vista' usually has a connotation of broad sweeping views ... but it's what popped into my head to describe this quiet fall scene,\" Craig writes. \"A more intimate definition of vista that invites one to walk into the picture.\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
