ize:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Think on your feet </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/DBtjOYN3OUyKngj5YklEa2H6mF4=/0x0:1920x1080/1920x1080/2026/10/26/1793036924831.jpeg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of a crosswalk light with a light bulb instead of a person walking. \"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Your next great idea</strong> probably won't come from sitting on the couch. You may need to take a walk, preferably without a destination, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></p><ul><li><strong>🚶 Why it matters: </strong>Walking doesn't just benefit your physical and mental health. Research shows it can unlock <em>divergent thinking — </em>the ability to generate new and original ideas that's essential to creativity. </li></ul><p><strong>🖼️ The big picture: </strong>Masters of their craft, from Albert Einstein to Stephen King and Virginia Woolf, all credited walking with fueling their creativity. Now, science is backing them up, <em>The Washington Post's Richard Sima <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cud2FzaGluZ3RvbnBvc3QuY29tL3dlbGxuZXNzLzIwMjYvMTAvMTgvYm9vc3QteW91ci1jcmVhdGl2aXR5LXRyeS10YWtpbmctd2Fsay8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB1d8bfc84\" target=\"_blank\">reports</a>.</em></p><ul><li><strong>A </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly9qb3VybmFscy5wbG9zLm9yZy9wbG9zb25lL2FydGljbGU_aWQ9MTAuMTM3MS9qb3VybmFsLnBvbmUuMDM0Nzg3OCZ1dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBa03b4df3\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>recent analysis</strong></a><strong> </strong>of 23 studies found that walking significantly improved people's ability to come up with novel ideas. </li><li><strong>But there's a catch: </strong>Walking was less effective at <em>convergent thinking </em>— the process of evaluating ideas and deciding which are worth pursuing. That may require a different approach, including collaboration with others. </li></ul><p><strong>🔎  Zoom in: </strong>How you walk also matters. </p><ul><li><strong>A </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly9saW5rLnNwcmluZ2VyLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlLzEwLjEwMDcvczAwNDI2LTAyMS0wMTYzNi13P3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBee7bfd7b\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>2022 study</strong></a> found that people who were allowed to roam freely generated more novel ideas than those confined to a predetermined walking path. </li><li><strong>Researchers suspect</strong> that movement gives our minds the space to wander, helping us break free from rigid thought patterns.</li><li><strong>In a separate </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly9wc3ljbmV0LmFwYS5vcmcvcmVjb3JkLzIwMjQtMjYwNDMtMDAxP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBe86f45b0\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>2024 study</strong></a>, researchers tracked 157 young adults over five days and found that those who took more steps before a creativity test came up with more original answers.</li></ul><p><strong>🎶 The intrigue: </strong>The connection goes beyond brainstorming. </p><ul><li><strong>People who are more</strong> physically active also tend to engage more in creative pursuits — like cooking, music and performing arts — and report more creative accomplishments.</li></ul><p><strong>💡  The bottom line: </strong>Next time you're in a rut, get up and wander, even if it's in the comfort of your own home. (For me, that's three rooms, but whatever works!) </p><ul><li><strong>Give yourself space</strong> to daydream, but keep your phone or a notepad handy to record flashes of inspiration. </li><li><strong>Once you've got your idea</strong>, you'll probably need to take a seat to see it through.</li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtMTI0YTkyODgtODJmOC00ODljLTg5NTQtYTBmNjVjODhkNDQwLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB63a30831\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48100597.437981/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-bor